q(8,r'What is the largest size of a subset of {1,...,25} in which no two distinct selected numbers have sum divisible by 6?',11,[(13,'Allow multiple numbers in self-complementary residue classes.'),(12,'Ignore one of the self-complementary restrictions.'),(10,'Discard both residue-zero and residue-three representatives unnecessarily.'),(9,'Omit the extra member in residue class one.')],[r'Classes 1 and 5 cannot both be represented; their sizes are five and four, so at most five can be taken from their union. Classes 2 and 4 similarly contribute at most four.',r'Within class zero, or within class three, any two distinct numbers have sum divisible by six, so each contributes at most one.',r'The bound is 5+4+1+1=11, attained by taking all residues 1 and 2 plus one number from each of classes 0 and 3.'],['Pair complementary residue classes.' ,'Residues zero and three are their own complements and need different treatment.'],'Optimize a residue-class packing with self-complementary cases.',lambda:max(sum(n%6==r for n in range(1,26)) for r in [1,5])+max(sum(n%6==r for n in range(1,26)) for r in [2,4])+2,'Independent class counts and explicit eleven-element witness.'),
q(9,r'Six coins lie in a row, initially all heads. A move flips two adjacent coins. How many configurations are reachable?',32,[(64,'Ignore the parity invariant.'),(16,'Impose an extra unsupported invariant.'),(6,'Count only one-move configurations and the start.'),(31,'Exclude the initial configuration.')],[r'Every move preserves the parity of the number of tails, so at most 2^5=32 configurations are reachable.',r'For any desired even-tail configuration, scan from left to right. Whenever a position is wrong, flip it with the next coin, fixing it permanently.',r'After the first five positions are fixed, parity forces the last to be correct. Thus every even-tail configuration is reachable.'],['Find an invariant first.' ,'Then give a procedure reaching every configuration that satisfies it.'],'Prove both necessity and sufficiency of a parity invariant.',lambda:reachable(6)),
q(10,r'What is the least number of people guaranteeing that among them are either three mutual acquaintances or three mutual strangers, assuming acquaintance is symmetric?',6,[(5,'Omit the five-cycle counterexample.'),(4,'Apply the pigeonhole principle one stage too early.'),(7,'Use a nonsharp guarantee.'),(3,'Assume any three people are homogeneous.')],[r'With six people, choose one person. At least three of the other five have the same acquaintance relation to that person.',r'If any two of those three share that relation, they form a homogeneous triple with the chosen person; otherwise the three themselves form a triple of the opposite relation.',r'Five people can avoid both: make acquaintance edges a five-cycle. Neither it nor its complement contains a triangle. Thus six is least.'],['Focus on the relations from one chosen person.' ,'For sharpness, search for a symmetric five-person counterexample.'],'Combine a local pigeonhole argument with a graph construction.',ramsey_three,'Exhaustive two-color graphs through six vertices; proof certificate: three same-colored incident edges force a monochromatic triangle; a five-cycle and its complement are triangle-free.'),
q(11,r'Distinct positive integers have sum 10. What is the largest possible product?',30,[(24,'Use 1,2,3,4 without comparing three-part choices.'),(36,'Use the repeated parts 3,3,4, violating distinctness.'),(40,'Use 2,4,5, whose sum exceeds ten.'),(27,'Use three equal threes, violating distinctness and the sum.')],[r'At most four distinct positive integers can be used because 1+2+3+4+5>10. Four force the set {1,2,3,4}, with product 24.',r'For three integers a<b<c, listing a=1 or 2 gives maximum 20 or 30, and a=3 is impossible because 3+4+5>10.',r'The largest triple is {2,3,5}, with product 30; compare two-part products, whose maximum is 4·6=24.'],['Bound the number of parts using the smallest distinct integers.' ,'For each number of parts, compare the feasible balanced choices.'],'Respect distinctness while proving a product maximum.',lambda:max(prod(t) for r in range(1,5) for t in combinations(range(1,11),r) if sum(t)==10)),
q(12,r'Start with the integers 1 through 10. Repeatedly replace two current numbers by their absolute difference until one remains. What is the largest possible final number?',9,[(10,'Use only the maximum bound and ignore parity.'),(55,'Assume the sum can survive subtraction.'),(1,'Assume all merge orders lead to the same result.'),(0,'Ignore the odd total-sum invariant.')],[r'No operation increases the largest entry, so the final number is at most ten. Replacing a,b by |a-b| changes the sum by an even number, so its final parity is that of 55: odd.',r'Thus the upper bound is nine. To attain it, pair 9 with 8, 7 with 6, 5 with 4, and 3 with 2, leaving five ones and ten.',r'Cancel pairs of ones to zero and absorb the zeros, leaving one and ten; their difference is nine.'],['Track both the largest entry and the sum modulo two.' ,'A bound is not enough: exhibit a sequence reaching it.'],'Combine a monotone bound, an invariant, and an attaining construction.',difference_witness,'Explicit move certificate checked against the initial multiset.'),
